        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost everyone attempting this underestimates it once, buys a general-purpose deck stripper, gets a patchy result, and concludes the product was bad. The product was fine. It was formulated for a completely different finish.
        </p>

        <div id="almost-paint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Solid Stain Is Almost Paint, and That Changes Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck finishes sit on a spectrum from fully penetrating to fully film-forming, and solid stain sits much closer to the paint end than its name suggests. A semi-transparent stain is mostly oil and pigment carried into the wood fibres, leaving very little on the surface. A solid stain is a pigmented acrylic that leaves a genuine film, thinner and more flexible than porch paint but a film nonetheless.
          </p>

          <p>
            That film is why it hides grain, why it lasts longer than semi-transparent, and why it fails by peeling rather than fading. It is also why removing it is a scraping job rather than a rinsing job. A stripper has to break the film all the way through, and then somebody has to push the softened material off the boards.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Finish</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where It Sits</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How It Fails</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Strip Difficulty</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">In the wood</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wears away</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Semi-transparent stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mostly in the wood</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fades</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Semi-solid stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Part film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fades, some peel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Harder</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin film on top</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Peels</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Deck paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thick film on top</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Peels, cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hardest</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical consequence is that every instruction written for stripping deck stain understates the job if what you have is solid. Dwell times need extending, product consumption roughly doubles, a second application is normal rather than exceptional, and the scraping is unavoidable. Our guide on stripping paint from a deck describes essentially the same process, and it is the closer match for what you are about to do.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one mercy. Solid stain is thinner and generally more permeable than paint, so it does release more readily than three coats of porch enamel. A deck with a single coat of solid stain that is already weathering and peeling in places can strip surprisingly well in one application. The difficulty scales sharply with coat count.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coat-count" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Work Out How Many Coats You Are Dealing With
          </h2>

          <p>
            This determines the product, the quantity, the number of rounds, and honestly whether the job is worth doing at all. It takes ten minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Find an edge where the finish has already failed, usually a step nose, a board end, or a high-traffic path where it has worn through. Look at the exposed edge of the film in raking light, or lift a flake and examine its cross-section. Distinct colour layers are visible to the naked eye surprisingly often, particularly where a previous owner changed shade.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you cannot see layers, do a test strip. Pick a square foot somewhere hidden, apply stripper at the product's stated rate, dwell the full time and scrape. What you find tells you everything: bare wood means one coat and a straightforward job; another colour means at least two and a second application; a stubborn base layer that will not lift means a deck that may be better sanded.
          </p>

          <p>
            While you are there, assess the wood itself. Push a screwdriver into a board near a joist and at a board end. If it sinks easily, that board is punky and no finish will rescue it. Solid stain is often applied precisely because it hides tired wood, so finding soft boards underneath is a common and important discovery to make before you commit a fortnight.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What the Test Patch Tells You</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1 coat</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">One application, moderate job</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2 coats</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Two applications, plan a weekend</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3+ coats</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Consider sanding or replacing</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Soft wood</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Stop; finish will not fix it</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Do not skip straight to buying. A deck with three coats of solid stain over fifteen years is a genuinely large project, and knowing that at the start lets you choose deliberately between stripping, sanding, recoating with solid stain again, or replacing the boards. All four are defensible; only the uninformed version is not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="product-strength" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why the Consumer Stripper Stalls on Solid Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Walk into a hardware store and most of what is labelled deck stripper is a sodium percarbonate product, sometimes called an oxygen or percarbonate stripper. It is mild, it is safe around plants, it does not darken wood much, and on semi-transparent stain it works well.
          </p>

          <p>
            On solid stain it frequently does not. Percarbonate works by oxidising and lifting pigment that is sitting in and lightly on the wood, and it is not a strong enough attack on a cured acrylic film. People apply it, see the surface go slightly dull, scrub, and get patchy removal that leaves a ghost of the old colour. That is the product working exactly as designed on a finish it was not designed for.
          </p>

          <p>
            For solid stain you generally want a sodium hydroxide based caustic stripper, ideally in a thickened or gel form so it clings and stays wet through a long dwell. Products marketed specifically for solid stain and deck paint are almost always caustic. Our comparison of caustic and oxygen strippers covers the trade-offs in detail, because caustic is not free: it darkens wood significantly, it demands a brightener step afterwards, and it is much harsher on plants, skin and metal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other specification that matters is viscosity. A thin liquid on horizontal decking in summer dries within fifteen minutes, which is less than solid stain needs. A gel holds moisture, can be covered with plastic sheet to extend the dwell substantially, and is the only thing that will stay put on railings and vertical faces long enough to work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy generously. Solid stain consumes roughly twice the stripper of semi-transparent per square foot, and running out partway through a section is genuinely costly: the treated area dries, the softened film sets back into the grain, and that patch becomes harder than it was originally. Our coverage guide on how much deck stripper you need runs the arithmetic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dwell" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Dwell Time, and the One Rule That Decides the Outcome
          </h2>

          <p>
            If there is a single sentence worth taking from this page: the stripper must stay wet for the whole dwell, and everything else is detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chemical strippers act only while wet. The moment the surface dries the reaction stops, and the partly dissolved film re-hardens onto and into the wood in a state that is more difficult to remove than the original finish. This is the mechanism behind almost every disappointing result, and it is entirely preventable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solid stain typically wants 15 to 30 minutes, longer with multiple coats. In direct August sun a thin application can be dry in ten. So: work in shade or on an overcast day, start early or late rather than at midday, and keep a pump sprayer of plain water to mist any area that starts to skin over. Misting extends the dwell without meaningfully diluting the product.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most effective single technique is covering the treated area with plastic sheeting. It stops evaporation almost completely, lets you hold a dwell of 45 minutes or more on a stubborn section, and costs a few pounds for a roll. Professionals use it routinely and homeowners almost never do, which is a large part of the difference in results.
          </p>

          <p>
            Equally, do not over-dwell. A caustic stripper left far beyond its window on softwood raises the grain severely, darkens the wood beyond what a brightener will fully reverse, and can leave a fuzzy surface needing sanding regardless. Watch the finish rather than the clock: you want it wrinkled, lifted or visibly gummy, and that is the moment to scrape.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Removal Technique
          </h2>

          <p>
            Work in sections sized to what you can scrape before the stripper dries, which for most people is 50 to 100 square feet. Apply, dwell, scrape, rinse, move on. Resist the urge to coat the whole deck first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scrape with the grain using a stiff putty knife or a proper scraper with a sharp edge, and dress the edge with a file regularly. A dull blade tears wood fibres instead of lifting softened film, and torn fibres are what produce the fuzzy finish people blame on the chemical. On grooved boards, a stiff-bristle brush reaches into the grooves where a blade rides over them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then rinse rather than blast. The pressure washer's job at this stage is removing residue and spent chemical, not doing the removal. Softwood is damaged above roughly 1,500 PSI and cedar below that, so use a 25 or 40 degree tip, hold it at least twelve inches off, move constantly and always with the grain. If paint or stain is not coming away, the answer is another application, never more pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect the second round to be patchy work rather than a full reapplication. Areas under a roof overhang, in shade, or in low traffic will have a thicker, better-preserved film than the sun-baked centre of the deck. Treat round two as spot treatment on what remains and you will use a fraction of the product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then brighten. A caustic strip leaves wood alkaline and visibly darkened, and both matter: alkaline wood takes stain unevenly and can react with it, and the darkening does not weather out. An oxalic or citric acid brightener neutralises and restores colour, and it is part of the chemistry rather than a cosmetic extra. Our guide on deck brightener versus deck cleaner explains why the two are not interchangeable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, let it dry properly. Two to three dry days is a realistic minimum after stripping and rinsing, longer in cool or humid weather. Test by sprinkling water on the boards: beading means the wood is still too wet or residue remains, and staining over either produces a finish that fails within a season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-sand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When to Stop Stripping and Sand Instead
          </h2>

          <p>
            Knowing when to abandon the chemical route is worth as much as knowing how to do it, and there is no shame in switching partway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switch to sanding if two full applications have left a stubborn base layer that will not lift. At that point you are usually looking at an old alkyd solid stain or a primer coat, and further chemical rounds have diminishing returns while continuing to darken and raise the grain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switch if the deck is small and flat. On a compact deck with simple square-edge railings, a floor sander or a good random orbit sander with 60 to 80 grit is often faster than chemical stripping, produces no runoff to manage, and leaves a surface ready for finish. Chemical stripping earns its place on large decks and on complex profiles, not on a tidy 10 by 12 platform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switch if runoff is a genuine problem. A deck over a patio, over planting beds you care about, or close to a watercourse makes caustic runoff a real issue. Sanding with dust collection is the cleaner answer in those situations even if it is more work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not switch to sanding on railings, spindles and balusters. Sanding a turned or moulded profile is slow, awkward and produces an uneven result, and this is precisely where chemical stripping is worth the mess. Most experienced deck refinishers end up doing exactly this hybrid: sand the field, strip the rails.
          </p>

          <p>
            And consider stopping altogether. If the wood underneath is soft, if there are three or more coats, or if the boards are near the end of their life, the honest comparison is between a fortnight of stripping and replacing the deck boards. Decking is not expensive relative to that much labour, and a new surface takes a finish far better than a rescued one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-next" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Put Back On
          </h2>

          <p>
            Having done this once, the choice of new finish is really a choice about when you will do it again.
          </p>

          <p>
            Going back to solid stain is legitimate and is the right answer when the wood is tired and you want it hidden, or when you want maximum colour coverage and the longest interval between recoats. Understand the commitment: solid stain fails by peeling, and in five to eight years you will be back at this page. Recoating solid over sound solid is straightforward, though, so the cycle is only this painful when adhesion has gone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moving to semi-transparent is what most people who have just stripped a deck decide to do, and it is the lower-maintenance path in practice. It fades rather than peels, which means recoating is a clean and a fresh coat rather than a strip. The catch is that it shows the wood, including any damage the solid stain was concealing and any ghosting left by an imperfect strip.
          </p>

          <p>
            That ghosting point is worth dwelling on. If your strip left traces of the old colour in the grain, semi-transparent will show them and solid will not. Be honest about the result you achieved before choosing, and if in doubt, wet a section with water to preview roughly how a translucent finish will look.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, apply it in the right conditions: wood fully dry, temperature within the product's range, and not in direct hot sun, which flashes the surface before it penetrates. Our guides on the best time of year to stain a deck and how many coats of deck stain to apply cover the timing and the build.
          </p>

          <p>
            One last practical note. Buy the finish before you finish the strip, and keep a record of the product and colour somewhere durable. The single biggest cause of a difficult strip is nobody knowing what was on the deck, and you are in a position to spare the next person that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Percarbonate Stripper for Solid Stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most hardware-store deck strippers are sodium percarbonate, which lifts pigment sitting in and lightly on the wood and is not a strong enough attack on a cured acrylic film. You get patchy removal and a ghost of the old colour. Solid stain generally needs a caustic, sodium hydroxide based product in a gel or thickened form.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Stripper Dry Before Scraping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Strippers act only while wet, and once the surface dries the partly dissolved film re-hardens into the grain in a state harder to remove than the original. Work 50 to 100 square feet at a time in shade, mist with water if it skins, and cover stubborn sections with plastic sheet to hold the dwell.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Test Patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ten minutes in a hidden square foot tells you the coat count, whether a second application is coming, and whether the wood underneath is sound. Solid stain is often applied to hide tired boards, so discovering soft wood after committing a fortnight to stripping is a genuinely common and avoidable outcome.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Raising the Pressure Washer to Finish the Job</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Softwood is damaged above roughly 1,500 PSI and cedar below that, and the fuzzed grain you create then needs sanding anyway. The washer removes residue; it does not do the stripping. If film remains, apply more stripper. Use a 25 or 40 degree tip at twelve inches, always with the grain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Before the Deck Has Dried Out</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A stripped and rinsed deck holds far more water than it looks, and stain applied to damp wood cannot penetrate and fails within a season. Allow two to three dry days minimum, longer in cool or humid weather, and test by sprinkling water: if it beads, the wood is still too wet or residue remains.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is solid stain harder to strip than regular deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Considerably. Solid stain is a pigmented acrylic that leaves a genuine film on the surface, closer in behaviour to thin paint than to a penetrating stain. It needs a stronger stripper, roughly double the product, a longer dwell, hands-on scraping, and frequently a second application.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What stripper removes solid deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A sodium hydroxide based caustic stripper, ideally as a gel or thickened liquid so it clings and stays wet. Products marketed specifically for solid stain or deck paint are almost always caustic. The mild percarbonate strippers that dominate hardware store shelves are formulated for semi-transparent stain and usually stall on solid.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do you leave stripper on solid stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically 15 to 30 minutes, longer with multiple coats, but watch the finish rather than the clock: you want it wrinkled, lifted or visibly gummy. Never let it dry, and do not massively over-dwell either, because caustic left too long on softwood raises grain and darkens the wood beyond what brightener fully reverses.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I just sand solid stain off instead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On flat, accessible boards and a small deck, often yes, and it avoids runoff entirely. Sanding is impractical on railings, spindles and moulded profiles, which is where chemical stripping earns its place. Many refinishers do a hybrid: sand the field boards, strip the rails.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know how many coats of solid stain are on my deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look at a failed edge, a step nose or a worn traffic path in raking light, where distinct colour layers are often visible. If not, strip a hidden square foot fully: bare wood means one coat, another colour means at least two, and a stubborn base layer that will not lift suggests sanding or replacement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to use a brightener after stripping solid stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                After a caustic stripper, yes. It leaves the wood alkaline and visibly darkened, and alkaline wood accepts stain unevenly and can react with it. An oxalic or citric acid brightener neutralises the surface and restores the colour, and the darkening does not weather out on its own.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I go back to solid stain or switch to semi-transparent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Semi-transparent is the lower-maintenance path because it fades rather than peels, so future recoats are a clean and a coat rather than another strip. The catch is that it shows everything the solid stain was hiding, including damaged wood and any ghosting your strip left behind. Wet a section with water to preview the effect.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

