        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Start with a test patch, because the coat count decides the whole project. One coat of weathering solid stain often comes off in a single application. Three coats over fifteen years is a fortnight of work, and knowing that at the outset lets you choose deliberately between stripping, sanding, recoating solid over solid, or replacing the boards.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you strip, use a caustic gel rather than the mild percarbonate product that dominates the shelves, work 50 to 100 square feet at a time, and keep it wet for the whole dwell — plastic sheeting over the treated area is the trick that separates good results from patchy ones. Then brighten to undo the alkalinity and darkening, and give the boards two to three dry days before anything else touches them.
          </p>
        </div>

